  "body": "## Problem\n\nThe built-in ER collets of #3898 (PR to follow, stacked on #4271) have one exact bore each: whole millimetres from the ER table (`holder_library::ER_SIZES`).\n\nThe fit rule `ConnectionCode::takes` compares a plain bore with a shank within 0.02 mm. Thus a collet takes only a shank of its nominal bore.\n\nA real ER collet clamps a range. The range goes from the nominal bore down by 0.5 mm (ER11) or 1 mm (ER16 and larger). For this reason, these shanks of the starter catalogue find no built-in collet:\n\n- the inch shanks 3.175 mm, 6.35 mm and 12.7 mm;\n- the drill shanks such as 4.2 mm, 6.8 mm and 10.2 mm.\n\n## Proposal\n\nGive a collet a clamping range on its tool side. Let the fit rule accept a shank in that range.\n\n- Put the clamping range of each series in the ER table, next to `min_bore` and `max_bore`.\n- Add a tool-side connection code with a range, or a collet-specific check in `component_fit`.\n- Keep the exact match for a plain bore that is not a collet (a shrink-fit or a hydraulic holder).\n\n## Done means\n\n- A 6.35 mm shank fits the `ER32 collet 7 mm`, and a 5.9 mm shank does not.\n- \"Show compatible parts\" on a built-in 1/4 in tool lists its collets.\n- The resolver tests stay green. A new test pins the range limits of each series.\n\nThe fit rule decides which parts the stack accepts. It is under the safety floor, so the issue is `effort:high`.\n",
